WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.251
Attackers are using artificial intelligence to impersonate people and make scams harder to spot.

00:00:10.251 --> 00:00:14.156
In this video, you’ll see how AI-powered deception can appear through

00:00:14.156 --> 00:00:19.054
voices, messages, profiles, and emotional pressure, and how to respond before you act.

00:00:19.054 --> 00:00:21.457
A message may look like it came from your manager.

00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:23.661
A voice may sound like your colleague.

00:00:23.661 --> 00:00:26.002
A profile may look like a real recruiter.

00:00:26.002 --> 00:00:28.327
But, what if none of them are real?

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:33.604
Today, AI can help attackers imitate the way real people write, speak, and interact.

00:00:33.604 --> 00:00:38.019
That means you can no longer rely only on whether something looks or sounds real.

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:42.673
A call can sound familiar.

00:00:42.873 --> 00:00:46.400
The voice uses the right tone, and the request feels urgent.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.650
“I need this approved now.” “Please send the file quickly.” “Can you keep this confidential?"

00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:55.673
Even if the voice sounds familiar, it may not be genuine.

00:00:55.673 --> 00:01:00.219
Attackers can use voice cloning or deepfake audio to impersonate someone you know.

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:05.495
The goal is not just to sound real; it is to make you act before you stop and check.

00:01:05.495 --> 00:01:09.936
If the request is unusual, verify it through a trusted channel before acting.

00:01:13.936 --> 00:01:17.593
AI-powered impersonation can also show up in your inbox.

00:01:17.593 --> 00:01:23.393
AI can help attackers create phishing emails that are clear, professional, and personalized.

00:01:23.393 --> 00:01:30.216
These emails can have no spelling mistakes, refer to real workplace context, and copy the tone of a trusted sender.

00:01:30.216 --> 00:01:34.338
So don’t rely only on poor grammar or obvious errors as warning signs.

00:01:34.338 --> 00:01:37.917
A phishing email can now look like normal business communication.

00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:44.317
Instead of judging only by how professional it looks, check the request, sender, link, and context.

00:01:50.317 --> 00:01:56.664
Attackers can also use AI to create fake profiles for recruiters, suppliers, employees, or professional contacts.

00:01:56.864 --> 00:01:58.384
The profile looks credible.

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:00.304
The message sounds natural.

00:02:00.504 --> 00:02:01.863
The conversation begins harmlessly.

00:02:02.063 --> 00:02:03.883
But over time, the request shifts.

00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:06.035
“Can you share this document?"

00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:07.986
“Can you open this link?"

00:02:08.186 --> 00:02:09.788
“Can you confirm your login details?"

00:02:09.788 --> 00:02:12.041
“Can we move this conversation to another platform?"

00:02:12.991 --> 00:02:15.577
The deception does not always happen all at once.

00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:22.029
It can build slowly across email, phone calls, video, chat, social media, or professional networking sites.

00:02:25.029 --> 00:02:28.504
The channel may change, but the tactic is often the same:

00:02:28.704 --> 00:02:30.796
Impersonate someone you trust.

00:02:30.996 --> 00:02:33.885
Create pressure and make verification feel unnecessary.

00:02:33.885 --> 00:02:36.393
That emotional pressure is the real hook.

00:02:36.393 --> 00:02:45.170
Attackers use urgency, fear, authority, curiosity, secrecy, or helpfulness to lower your guard.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:52.138
“Act now.” “Something is wrong.” “This is confidential.” “Can you help me quickly?"

00:02:53.137 --> 00:02:57.343
These tactics work because they push you to respond before you think.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:03:00.869
When a request creates pressure, pause before you act.

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:02.289
Ask yourself these questions.

00:03:02.489 --> 00:03:05.310
Is this urgent, emotional, or secretive?

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.333
Is it asking for money, access, documents, or credentials?

00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:11.789
Is it asking me to bypass a normal process?

00:03:11.789 --> 00:03:14.479
Would this person normally ask me this way?

00:03:14.479 --> 00:03:18.319
If the request feels unusual, treat that as a signal to verify.

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:21.062
Verify through a trusted source before you act.

00:03:21.062 --> 00:03:24.432
Call back using a known number, not the number in the message.

00:03:24.432 --> 00:03:29.813
Use agreed verification steps, such as an established protocol or secondary approval.

00:03:29.813 --> 00:03:33.940
Check sender details, links, attachments, and profile information carefully.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:39.217
Do not rely only on voice, video, caller ID, or how convincing a message appears.

00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:41.490
And if something still feels wrong, report it.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:45.852
[The lesson is simple]: if a request feels unusual, pause before you trust it.

00:03:45.852 --> 00:03:50.398
Verify through a trusted channel, follow the process, and report anything suspicious.

00:03:50.398 --> 00:03:54.002
With AI-powered deception, trust should never be automatic.
